{
  "configuration_id": "config_010",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_022 (rna_only, scgpt, multiprofile, avgpool) achieves 0.530 acc / 0.515 F1, while config_013 (multimodal, scgpt, resnet50, multiprofile, avgpool, concat) achieves only 0.512 acc / 0.477 F1. The ~1.8% accuracy and ~3.8% F1 gap suggests the image modality, as currently fused, is degrading performance rather than contributing useful signal. Question tested: config_010 changes exactly one variable from config_013—fusion from concat to weighted—while keeping scgpt, resnet50, multiprofile, and avgpool fixed. This isolates whether the concat fusion (which implicitly treats both modalities symmetrically) is the bottleneck, or whether the image encoder/aggregation itself is the problem. Why this is the best next experiment: it is the most parsimonious single-variable intervention most likely to close the gap if fusion is the culprit, since weighted fusion can learn to downweight the weaker image stream in favor of the stronger RNA signal. If it improves performance, we gain a promising direction to exploit; if it does not, we cleanly rule out fusion as the primary issue and can redirect to testing the image encoder (config_017) or aggregation (config_006) next, having eliminated one confound."
}

